<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">General Council of Andorra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1419">1419</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Consell General</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The General Council, or Consell General, is the parliament of Andorra. It developed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-la-terra">Consell de la Terra</a>, an assembly of parish representatives created in 1419, and from 1702 met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-la-vall">Casa de la Vall</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andorra-la-vella">Andorra la Vella</a>. The constitution of 1993 made it a full legislature: its 28 members, half elected by the parishes and half nationally, choose the head of government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/general-council-of-andorra.jpg" alt="General Council of Andorra" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lastjourneyman. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9th_General_Council_of_Andorra.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first parliament of Andorra was established in 1419 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-la-terra">Consell de la Terra</a>. Its councillors were elected by the population, and the council appointed syndics to manage the administration of the principality. It remained in force for several hundred years but slowly became the fiefdom of a few major families, which caused popular discontent by the nineteenth century. The General Council is sometimes called the General Council of the Valleys, its historical name, to distinguish it from similarly named bodies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-daran">Val d'Aran</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Major reforms were instituted in 1866, masterminded by Guillem d'Areny-Plandolit. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-la-terra">Consell de la Terra</a> was abolished and replaced by the Consell General de les Valls, with a syndic and a vice-syndic; the franchise was extended to all heads of families; and regular elections were laid down, with twelve of the then twenty-four councillors elected every two years. In the 1930s a dispute arose when the council began regularly to call Andorra a republic, which angered the co-princes. They ordered the council dissolved in June 1933 and a special election held, and the voting laws were changed so that all men over twenty-five could vote and all men over thirty stand.</p><p>In 1970 the vote was extended to women over twenty-five, and in 1971 the voting age was lowered to twenty-one and the age of candidates to twenty-five. Women gained the right to run for office in 1973, and a referendum on further reform was held in 1978. Later that year a seventh parish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/escaldes-engordany">Escaldes-Engordany</a>, was formed, bringing the number of councillors to twenty-eight. In 1982 an Executive Council was created, and in 1984 Mercè Bonell became its first female councillor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Council is a unicameral parliament of 28 general councillors elected for four-year terms on party lists. Two come from each of the seven parishes, elected from the list with most votes in the parish, and fourteen are elected from national lists by the largest remainder method of proportional representation. Voters cast two separate ballots, and the same person cannot appear on both a parish and a national list. Originally each parish returned four deputies, but as parishes varied in population from 350 to 2,500 this was felt to be imbalanced, and the national list was introduced for the 1997 elections.</p><p>The Council appoints a presiding officer, the Síndic general, and a deputy, the subsíndic. It elects the Head of Government, subject to approval of the Co-Princes, and the Head of Government presides over the Executive Council and appoints its other seven members. Political parties are a recent innovation: the first was formed in 1976 and parties gained legal recognition only in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “General Council (Andorra)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1419 the co-princes allowed the creation of the Consell de la Terra, an assembly of parish representatives that developed into the <b>Consell General</b>, which from 1702 met in the Casa de la Vall in Andorra la Vella.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
